#!/usr/bin/env python3
"""minimal-port: the mechanical parts of a minimal port (process/minimal-port.md): the sites report, the repin and
the source rewrite, from the rule data in minimal.rules (rules/minimal-port.toml).

usage:
  minimal-port sites   [--tree PATH] [--rules FILE] [--config PORT.TOML]
  minimal-port repin   [--tree PATH] [--rev REV] [--rules FILE] [--apply] [--config PORT.TOML]
  minimal-port rewrite [--tree PATH] [--rules FILE] [--only CLASS,...] [--skip CLASS,...] [--apply] [--config ...]

TREE defaults to app.integration. Every git-tracked .rs file and Cargo.toml of the tree is read; matching sees code
only (strings, chars and comments are masked).

sites    Lists every port item the rules' [[site]] patterns find, by runbook item, with file:line; per crate counts;
         the lines of each file with a dock site; the web shells; and the writer split: `all` (one writer) when no
         dock site is found, else `host` and `dock`. Writes sites.md and sites.json. Changes nothing. Exit 0 when no
         site is found (the app needs only the repin), 1 when port items exist.
repin    Rewrites the manifests onto REV (default plan.base):
           revs      every framework dependency to `git = framework.repo, rev = REV` (land/pin-framework.py's rule);
                     each `[patch.crates-io]` entry gets the rev the framework's own `[patch.crates-io]` at REV (read
                     with git show in framework.checkout) gives that crate, or, for a crate the framework does not
                     patch, the one rev the framework takes from the same git URL (an egui fork's other crates); an
                     entry the framework patches from another URL, or whose URL the framework does not use, is listed;
           features  the [[feature]] changes on single-line dependency entries and `features` lines of dependency
                     tables; a features array over several lines is listed.
         Cargo.lock is not touched: the first build resolves it.
rewrite  Rewrites the sources, by class:
           combo     [combo] old -> new, except a builder chain (to the end of its statement or argument) that calls
                     a keep_if_calls method: left and listed;
           <module>  each [[module]] class (dock, split, keymap): `use OLD::{..}` and `use OLD::Name` (pub and
                     aliases kept) split into the new paths, qualified OLD::Name paths, `Type::Variant` renames, and
                     bare renames of a name whose last segment changes in every file that imported it from OLD. A
                     file that already binds the new name imports it `as` the old name instead (listed); a renamed
                     `pub use` is listed, since its users elsewhere name the old name;
           deps      each [[dependency]] crate added to the manifest of every crate whose files name `when` and that
                     lacks it, modelled on that manifest's first `model` line (a `workspace = true` line also adds
                     the root's [workspace.dependencies] entry, modelled on the root's line); other forms are listed.
         --only and --skip take class names.

Without --apply, repin and rewrite print the diff and change nothing (a dry run). --apply refuses a tree with
uncommitted changes, writes the edits and commits once: `minimal-port <cmd>: <n> edits (<class> <n>, ...)`. A second
run finds nothing to change. Output in <work.runs>/minimal/: <cmd>.diff, <cmd>.json ({"tool", "cmd", "tree",
"applied", "edits": {class: n}, "files", "listed": [{"class", "file", "line", "text"}], "notes", "commit", "exit"});
a [[feature]] note goes to "notes" and is printed, without counting as listed. For sites
sites.md and sites.json ({"rules", "items": {item: [{"file", "line", "text", "note"}]}, "crates": {crate: n},
"dock_files": {file: lines}, "web_shells": [file], "writers": [...]}). Last stdout line:
  minimal-port <cmd>: <n> edits (...); <n> listed     |     minimal-port sites: <n> sites (<item> <n>, ...); writers ...
Exit 0 done with nothing listed, 1 when something is listed for the writer, 2 on a usage or tool failure.
"""
